/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  postDict -- the SIZING pass of the plant (no costing: the case study gives
  no costs and Choupo has no Turton set for a membrane skid, a work exchanger
  or a vessel bank).

  A PLANT OF SIX TRAINS (DEV.md 4c C199).  The Toray case study states six
  skids of 16 442 m3/day each, and a large SWRO plant is built exactly so: N
  identical trains, each with its own high-pressure pump, its own array of
  pressure exchangers and its own vessel racks (the practice read for C199:
  Danfoss, multiple trains for large SWRO plants; Sulzer MSD-RO for trains of
  20 000-30 000 m3/day).  The flowsheet is solved ONCE at plant flow -- six
  identical trains in parallel carry six times a train's flow, and the physics
  is the same -- and the sizing is done PER TRAIN: `trains` below declares the
  count with its source, and every entry that says `perTrain yes;` yields six
  items (train1 .. train6), each publishing its per-train numbers beside the
  plant's.  The flowsheet's `vessels 1398` is 6 x 233 and its three pumps and
  the exchanger carry the six trains' flow.

  WHAT IS READ, WHAT IS DECLARED.  Every pump and bank number the sizer does
  not READ from the run is the case's, declared with its origin (`literature`
  where an open page states it, `assumed` where none does) and where it came
  from.  The sources read for C199 (2026-11-02), by what each was and what was
  taken:
    * Sulzer, MSD-RO axially split multistage pump, brochure e10110 (the PDF
      read in full) and the product page: "covering SWRO train capacities from
      20 000 to 30 000 m3/d"; capacities 1000 to 1600 m3/h; pressures up to
      90 bar (the product page: up to 1600 m3/h, 650 m, 90 bar).  Taken:
      1600 m3/h and 90 bar as the largest high-pressure pump of one make.
    * Energy Recovery, PX Q-series page: "Operates with concentrate flow from
      4.5-147.6 m3/h (20-650 gpm)" and "up to 83 bar (1,200 psi)"; the PX Q650
      press release (9 March 2026) gives its range as 250-650 gpm.  Taken:
      147.6 m3/h = 650 gpm x 0.22712 m3/h per US gpm; 56.8 m3/h = 250 gpm x
      0.22712; 83 bar.
    * Danfoss, "Multiple trains for large SWRO plants -- how much flow should
      one pump produce": the page returned 403 to this reader (only the
      search engine's summary of it was read, which says a large plant is three
      or more parallel trains, each on a pump of 10 000-15 000 m3/day); used as
      context, NOT as a number in this case.
    * CompositesWorld, "Composites' role in seawater reverse osmosis
      desalination": Carlsbad has 2236 filament-wound pressure vessels of
      eight elements each, rated for different pressures in different areas
      (1200, 600 or 450 psi); the same page gives 8-inch, 28.4-foot vessels
      for the SANTA BARBARA plant.  The commander's C199 note attributed the
      28.4 ft to Carlsbad; the page says Santa Barbara.  Taken: the 28.4 ft
      length less eight 40-inch elements (8 x 1.016 m = 8.128 m) is 0.53 m of
      end hardware -- used as the vessel end allowance, ASSUMED to apply to
      this plant's seven-element vessel.
    * Element loading (Hydranautics TSB122, LG Chem TSB 102 read as search
      results only): no open source stated a numeric clearance at the vessel
      end, so the loading clearance is ASSUMED, not read.

  THE COUNTS AND THEIR CHECKS (recomputed by the case's gate from these dicts):
    pumps per train = ceil( Q_train / the largest pump ); the PX devices per
    train = ceil( Q_LP,train / 147.6 m3/h ), each between 56.8 and 147.6 m3/h
    and under 83 bar; vessel length = elements x 40 in + the end allowance;
    racks = ceil( vessels per train / vessels per rack ).

  THE THREE TANKS (the two contact tanks and the product store) are the
  flowsheet's `storageTank` units, linked by `unit <name>;` (DEV.md 4c C87):
  the hold time stays in the flowsheet.  Carbon steel stands in for the
  concrete or lined steel of a real potable-water store: it only fixes the
  record the sizer needs; no cost is computed.  The `material SS316` of the
  pumps and banks likewise fixes a record only (the vessels are FRP).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

sizing
{
    trains
    {
        count   6;
        source  "Toray case study MC-CS-CAMPODEDALIAS-1910: six skids of 16 442 m3/d each (6 x 16 442 = 98 652 against the 98 664 m3/d it states)";
        reason  "the skids are one design repeated (233 vessels of seven elements in the first pass, 54:18 in the second): identical trains in parallel, each with its own pump, exchanger array and racks, carry six times a train's flow, so the flowsheet is solved once at plant flow and sized per train";
    }

    units
    (
        //  ---- THE PUMPS: a pump is rated by the largest flow one machine of
        //  its make carries; the sheet publishes how many stand in parallel
        //  per train and in the plant, their utilisation and shaft power.
        {
            unitName IntakePump;  type ratedMachine;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                kind volumeRate;
                rating
                {
                    maxFlowPerUnit  4000 m3/h;
                    origin          assumed;
                    source          "author-set order of magnitude for a low-lift intake pump; no open source read states one (the Sulzer MSD-RO figures are for high-pressure membrane-feed pumps)";
                    reason          "the intake lifts the whole plant's water 1 bar to the filters; one machine cannot carry 9400 m3/h, so a small station of pumps in parallel stands in";
                }
                installed { count 3;  source "ceil(9386 m3/h / 4000 m3/h) = 3";  reason "the number of machines the declared maximum needs; the plant has ONE intake station (not per train)"; }
                spare     { count 1;  source "ASSUMED: one stand-by machine for the station, as the C199 practice reading says Carlsbad runs its high-pressure pumps (not re-read here)";  reason "a pump out for maintenance must not stop the plant"; }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName HPpump;  type ratedMachine;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                kind volumeRate;
                perTrain yes;
                rating
                {
                    maxFlowPerUnit  1600 m3/h;
                    pressureRating  90 bar;
                    origin          literature;
                    source          "Sulzer MSD-RO axially split multistage pump, product page and brochure e10110: capacities 1000 to 1600 m3/h, up to 90 bar, covering SWRO trains of 20 000-30 000 m3/d";
                    reason          "the largest high-pressure SWRO pump of one make read; this train's pump carries about 800 m3/h, half of it";
                }
                spare { count 1;  source "ASSUMED: one stand-by pump for the plant's six running pumps; the C199 practice reading says Carlsbad has 3 running and 1 spare (Ebara), not re-read here";  reason "a stand-by high-pressure pump that any train can switch to"; }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName Booster;  type ratedMachine;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                kind volumeRate;
                perTrain yes;
                rating
                {
                    maxFlowPerUnit  1600 m3/h;
                    pressureRating  90 bar;
                    origin          assumed;
                    source          "the Sulzer MSD-RO figures (1600 m3/h, 90 bar) taken as the ceiling of a booster of the same family; no open source read states a booster's own maximum";
                    reason          "the booster restores the pressure the exchanger loses (about 24 m of head) on the recovered flow of a train";
                }
                spare { count 1;  source "ASSUMED, as for the high-pressure pumps";  reason "a stand-by booster for the plant"; }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName Pass2Pump;  type ratedMachine;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                kind volumeRate;
                perTrain yes;
                rating
                {
                    maxFlowPerUnit  1600 m3/h;
                    pressureRating  90 bar;
                    origin          assumed;
                    source          "the Sulzer MSD-RO ceiling (1600 m3/h) taken for a second-pass feed pump at 10.7 bar; no open source read states a maximum for a pump of this duty";
                    reason          "the second-pass feed of a train, about 790 m3/h";
                }
                spare { count 1;  source "ASSUMED, as for the high-pressure pumps";  reason "a stand-by second-pass pump for the plant"; }
            }
        }

        //  ---- THE ENERGY-RECOVERY ARRAY: one array per train.  The case
        //  study's device is a piston-type work exchanger (DWEER); this
        //  engine's `pressureExchanger` is the rotary one, so the devices are
        //  counted from the Energy Recovery PX Q650's flow window, a stand-in
        //  that fixes the item's identity and the order of the count, not a
        //  statement about the real plant's devices.
        {
            unitName PX;  type pressureExchanger;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain          yes;
                ratedFlowPerUnit  147.6 m3/h;       // 650 gpm x 0.22712 m3/h per gpm: the top of the Q650 window
                minFlowPerUnit    56.8 m3/h;        // 250 gpm x 0.22712: the bottom of the Q650 window
                pressureRating    83 bar;           // the PX Q-series maximum
                source            "Energy Recovery PX Q-series page (4.5-147.6 m3/h, 20-650 gpm; up to 83 bar) and the PX Q650 press release of 9 March 2026 (250-650 gpm); a manufacturer's figures; the case study's own device is a piston-type work exchanger, so the count is a stand-in, not the plant's";
                pressureDesign    75 bar;           // author-set: the high-pressure inlet is 58 bar
            }
        }

        //  ---- THE VESSEL BANKS: the unit's `vessels` and `elements` become
        //  racks of horizontal vessels, per train and per pass.  Every
        //  number below is the case's and ASSUMED (no open source gives a
        //  rack pitch or a loading clearance); the vessel's end allowance is
        //  derived from a stated vessel length, see the header.
        {
            unitName RO1;  type membraneBank;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain yes;
                vessel
                {
                    endAllowance     0.53 m;
                    outsideDiameter  0.25 m;
                    origin           assumed;
                    source           "end allowance: a 28.4 ft (8.66 m) 8-inch vessel (CompositesWorld, Santa Barbara plant) less eight 40-inch elements = 0.53 m, assumed to hold for this plant's vessel; outside diameter: ASSUMED for an 8-inch class vessel with its end closures, none read";
                    reason           "the vessel is longer than its elements by the end closures and the feed and brine ports";
                }
                rack
                {
                    vesselsPerRack  48;
                    tiers           4;
                    pitch           0.40 m;
                    tierPitch       0.40 m;
                    baseHeight      0.30 m;
                    endMargin       0.25 m;
                    aisle           1.5 m;
                    origin          assumed;
                    source          "author-set round numbers; no open source gave a vessel pitch, rack size or aisle (DEV.md 4c C199 searched)";
                    reason          "four tiers of twelve vessels is a frame a person can service from the floor and the side; the pitch leaves room for the end-closure piping and couplings";
                }
                loadingClearance
                {
                    vesselLengths  1;
                    origin         assumed;
                    source         "ASSUMED: the elements are pushed into a vessel from one end, so a free length of one vessel is kept at that end (the practice the C199 brief states); the Hydranautics and LG Chem loading bulletins were found as search results only and state no clearance";
                    reason         "elements are pushed in and out one at a time, in line with the vessel";
                }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName RO2a;  type membraneBank;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain yes;
                vessel
                {
                    endAllowance     0.53 m;
                    outsideDiameter  0.25 m;
                    origin           assumed;
                    source           "as RO1";
                    reason           "as RO1: the same vessel family";
                }
                rack
                {
                    vesselsPerRack  28;
                    tiers           4;
                    pitch           0.40 m;
                    tierPitch       0.40 m;
                    baseHeight      0.30 m;
                    endMargin       0.25 m;
                    aisle           1.5 m;
                    origin          assumed;
                    source          "as RO1 (author-set)";
                    reason          "54 vessels per train in two racks of 28 and 26: four tiers of seven";
                }
                loadingClearance { vesselLengths 1;  origin assumed;  source "as RO1";  reason "as RO1"; }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName RO2b;  type membraneBank;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain yes;
                vessel
                {
                    endAllowance     0.53 m;
                    outsideDiameter  0.25 m;
                    origin           assumed;
                    source           "as RO1";
                    reason           "as RO1: the same vessel family";
                }
                rack
                {
                    vesselsPerRack  20;
                    tiers           4;
                    pitch           0.40 m;
                    tierPitch       0.40 m;
                    baseHeight      0.30 m;
                    endMargin       0.25 m;
                    aisle           1.5 m;
                    origin          assumed;
                    source          "as RO1 (author-set)";
                    reason          "18 vessels per train in one rack: four tiers of five";
                }
                loadingClearance { vesselLengths 1;  origin assumed;  source "as RO1";  reason "as RO1"; }
            }
        }

        //  ---- THE HEADERS (DEV.md 4c C199, the header sizer): the feed,
        //  brine and permeate header of each vessel bank, sized per train by
        //  a velocity window from the flow of the stream each carries.  The
        //  window and the list of bores are the case's and ASSUMED; see the
        //  design record docs/design/a-header-is-sized-by-its-velocity.md.
        {
            unitName RO1;  type pipeHeader;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain yes;
                headers
                (
                { role feed; stream roFeed; }
                { role brine; stream brine; }
                { role permeate; stream perm1; }
                );
                frictionModel Churchill;
                velocity
                {
                    min     1.0 m/s;
                    max     2.0 m/s;
                    origin  assumed;
                    source  "typical 1-2 m/s of a commercial RO plant's piping (Netsol Water, 'How to design pipe size in a commercial RO plant', read 2026-11-02; a vendor page for small plants, not specific to seawater or to a header); no open SWRO source gave a window (searched: FilmTec design guidelines, vendor pages)";
                    reason  "the first pass carries a whole train's flow in each header: a window typical of plant piping keeps the head loss and the erosion at the branch connections modest";
                }
                nominalSizes
                {
                    origin  assumed;
                    source  "author-set list of inside diameters, standing for the bores the plant can buy; no pipe schedule table is carried (ASME B36.10, DIN and ISO tables are no-grant standards)";
                    reason  "the sizer needs a list to choose from; the list is the case's, not a standard's";
                    sizes
                    (
                        { insideDiameter 0.15 m; } { insideDiameter 0.20 m; } { insideDiameter 0.25 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.30 m; } { insideDiameter 0.35 m; } { insideDiameter 0.40 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.45 m; } { insideDiameter 0.50 m; } { insideDiameter 0.55 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.60 m; } { insideDiameter 0.70 m; } { insideDiameter 0.80 m; }
                    );
                }
                roughness
                {
                    value   0.045 mm;
                    source  "the textbook roughness of commercial steel (0.045 mm, Moody chart); not read from a source for this pipe's material";
                    reason  "an author-set value for a clean header; the real wall (duplex steel, GRP) is smoother or rougher by the maker's figure";
                }
                viscosity
                {
                    value   1.0e-3 Pa.s;
                    source  "ASSUMED seawater near 20 C, the same value the case's modules use; the package declares no liquid-viscosity model";
                    reason  "the Reynolds number needs one; the drop per metre is insensitive to it in rough turbulent flow";
                }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName RO2a;  type pipeHeader;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain yes;
                headers
                (
                { role feed; stream feed2; }
                { role brine; stream conc2a; }
                { role permeate; stream perm2a; }
                );
                frictionModel Churchill;
                velocity
                {
                    min     1.0 m/s;
                    max     2.0 m/s;
                    origin  assumed;
                    source  "typical 1-2 m/s of a commercial RO plant's piping (Netsol Water, 'How to design pipe size in a commercial RO plant', read 2026-11-02; a vendor page for small plants, not specific to seawater or to a header); no open SWRO source gave a window (searched: FilmTec design guidelines, vendor pages)";
                    reason  "as RO1: the second pass's first stage";
                }
                nominalSizes
                {
                    origin  assumed;
                    source  "author-set list of inside diameters, standing for the bores the plant can buy; no pipe schedule table is carried (ASME B36.10, DIN and ISO tables are no-grant standards)";
                    reason  "the sizer needs a list to choose from; the list is the case's, not a standard's";
                    sizes
                    (
                        { insideDiameter 0.15 m; } { insideDiameter 0.20 m; } { insideDiameter 0.25 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.30 m; } { insideDiameter 0.35 m; } { insideDiameter 0.40 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.45 m; } { insideDiameter 0.50 m; } { insideDiameter 0.55 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.60 m; } { insideDiameter 0.70 m; } { insideDiameter 0.80 m; }
                    );
                }
                roughness
                {
                    value   0.045 mm;
                    source  "the textbook roughness of commercial steel (0.045 mm, Moody chart); not read from a source for this pipe's material";
                    reason  "an author-set value for a clean header; the real wall (duplex steel, GRP) is smoother or rougher by the maker's figure";
                }
                viscosity
                {
                    value   1.0e-3 Pa.s;
                    source  "ASSUMED seawater near 20 C, the same value the case's modules use; the package declares no liquid-viscosity model";
                    reason  "the Reynolds number needs one; the drop per metre is insensitive to it in rough turbulent flow";
                }
            }
        }
        {
            unitName RO2b;  type pipeHeader;  material SS316;
            designRules
            {
                perTrain yes;
                headers
                (
                { role feed; stream conc2a; }
                { role brine; stream conc2; }
                { role permeate; stream perm2b; }
                );
                frictionModel Churchill;
                velocity
                {
                    min     1.0 m/s;
                    max     2.0 m/s;
                    origin  assumed;
                    source  "typical 1-2 m/s of a commercial RO plant's piping (Netsol Water, 'How to design pipe size in a commercial RO plant', read 2026-11-02; a vendor page for small plants, not specific to seawater or to a header); no open SWRO source gave a window (searched: FilmTec design guidelines, vendor pages)";
                    reason  "as RO1: the second pass's second stage, fed with the first stage's concentrate";
                }
                nominalSizes
                {
                    origin  assumed;
                    source  "author-set list of inside diameters, standing for the bores the plant can buy; no pipe schedule table is carried (ASME B36.10, DIN and ISO tables are no-grant standards)";
                    reason  "the sizer needs a list to choose from; the list is the case's, not a standard's";
                    sizes
                    (
                        { insideDiameter 0.15 m; } { insideDiameter 0.20 m; } { insideDiameter 0.25 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.30 m; } { insideDiameter 0.35 m; } { insideDiameter 0.40 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.45 m; } { insideDiameter 0.50 m; } { insideDiameter 0.55 m; }
                        { insideDiameter 0.60 m; } { insideDiameter 0.70 m; } { insideDiameter 0.80 m; }
                    );
                }
                roughness
                {
                    value   0.045 mm;
                    source  "the textbook roughness of commercial steel (0.045 mm, Moody chart); not read from a source for this pipe's material";
                    reason  "an author-set value for a clean header; the real wall (duplex steel, GRP) is smoother or rougher by the maker's figure";
                }
                viscosity
                {
                    value   1.0e-3 Pa.s;
                    source  "ASSUMED seawater near 20 C, the same value the case's modules use; the package declares no liquid-viscosity model";
                    reason  "the Reynolds number needs one; the drop per metre is insensitive to it in rough turbulent flow";
                }
            }
        }
    );

    storage
    (
        { unit Remineralisation;  material carbonSteel;  maxTankVolume 5000; }
        { unit Disinfection;      material carbonSteel;  maxTankVolume 5000; }
        { unit ProductTank;       material carbonSteel;  maxTankVolume 10000; }
    );
}
